// Expected: c1 at one session and at two.
//
// The counterweight to cap_forgeable_is_not_a_reuse_pair.vp. Alice seals two
// messages under one key and one nonce; the attacker knows the first
// plaintext and not the second. That is a genuine reuse pair — both
// ciphertexts came out of the real encryption function — so the second
// plaintext follows, and it follows whether or not `forgeable` is declared on
// the first call. Drop the annotation and the verdict must not move.
//
// This is what stops the mint refusal from being read as "mintable members
// are never paired": a ciphertext whose plaintext the attacker happens to
// hold is mintable under the assumption and is still a real ciphertext, so
// `protocol_produced` keeps it. Refusing on mintability alone lost this
// attack the moment the assumption was declared, which the `weaken` property
// forbids.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	knows public m1, n, ad
	knows private m2
	e1 = AEAD_ENC[forgeable](k, n, m1, ad)
	e2 = AEAD_ENC(k, n, m2, ad)
]

Alice -> Bob: e1, e2

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	knows public n, ad
	d = AEAD_DEC(k, n, e2, ad)?
]

queries[
	confidentiality? m2
]
